WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:06.864
This is Chapter Two, Episode Six, Part Two.

3
00:00:07.414 --> 00:00:10.752
Fourth, interest organisations and associations.

4
00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:18.427
In Denmark, there is a tradition that politicians involve associations and interest organisations in political decisions.

5
00:00:18.427 --> 00:00:30.789
The relevant organisations are heard and try to make their views known to the ministries, for example by writing or coming to meetings, when new legislation that affects their interests and members is being worked on.

6
00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:35.539
They can also, by agreement, show up at the Folketing's various committees.

7
00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:41.002
Interest organisations in that way seek to gain influence and to influence the political agenda.

8
00:00:41.002 --> 00:00:45.327
But they also often contribute useful knowledge to the political process.

9
00:00:45.327 --> 00:00:50.754
Interest organisations are often part of committees and working groups set up by the government.

10
00:00:51.304 --> 00:00:58.041
Interest organisations arose at the same time as the political parties, at the end of the 1800s.

11
00:00:57.992 --> 00:01:01.356
They continue to play an active role in political life.

12
00:01:01.906 --> 00:01:07.606
In the beginning there were employers' associations, trade unions and farmers' associations.

13
00:01:07.556 --> 00:01:17.981
While the parties took care of the political fight, the interest organisations focused more narrowly on their members' interests, including their economic conditions and working conditions.

14
00:01:17.981 --> 00:01:25.731
In this period and well into the 1900s, there were close ties between parties and interest organisations.

15
00:01:25.731 --> 00:01:39.856
The Social Democrats cooperated closely with the Danish Confederation of Trade Unions, LO, the largest main organisation for trade unions in Denmark, which is now called the Danish Trade Union Confederation, FH.

16
00:01:39.856 --> 00:01:52.769
The Conservative People's Party cooperated with the Danish Employers' Confederation, DA, which is the organisation of the employers, while LO, now FH, is the main organisation for the trade unions.

17
00:01:52.769 --> 00:02:01.469
And Venstre with the Agricultural Council, Landbrugsrådet, which is now called Landbrug og Fødevarer, Agriculture and Food.

18
00:02:01.469 --> 00:02:08.119
Through the last roughly 25 years, the close ties between parties and organisations have become looser.

19
00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:13.062
All interest organisations seek to influence most of the parties in the Folketing.

20
00:02:13.612 --> 00:02:16.875
Many new interest organisations have also come along.

21
00:02:16.825 --> 00:02:22.850
Today, interest organisations and associations cover nearly all areas of political life.

22
00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:30.264
The old organisations have been supplemented by a number of new ones within areas such as climate, health and education.

23
00:02:30.814 --> 00:02:33.802
Fifth, the media and the public debate.

24
00:02:33.751 --> 00:02:38.476
The media play an important role for democracy in Denmark in several ways.

25
00:02:38.476 --> 00:02:48.739
On the one hand, radio, TV, newspapers, magazines and social media and so on help bring the political debate out to a wider circle of citizens.

26
00:02:48.739 --> 00:02:55.952
On the other hand, the media also function as channels where citizens can contribute to the debate with their opinions.

27
00:02:55.952 --> 00:03:00.214
Finally, the media can themselves influence the political agenda.

28
00:03:00.214 --> 00:03:04.434
In all three ways they act as a link between politicians and voters.

29
00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:08.121
The Danish media are independent of the government.

30
00:03:08.071 --> 00:03:12.884
They can therefore freely debate and take a critical view of the political agenda.

31
00:03:12.884 --> 00:03:19.071
Earlier, a number of Danish newspapers functioned as mouthpieces for the political parties.

32
00:03:19.071 --> 00:03:23.196
They were, in other words, controlled by party politics.

33
00:03:23.196 --> 00:03:36.109
Even in many smaller towns, four newspapers were published, which represented the four old parties: the Social Democrats, Venstre, the Social Liberal Party and the Conservative People's Party.

34
00:03:36.109 --> 00:03:42.272
The newspapers faithfully reproduced the party's views, and the readers were often members of the party.

35
00:03:42.272 --> 00:03:49.046
During the 1960s, newspapers changed character and freed themselves from the political parties.

36
00:03:49.046 --> 00:03:54.434
Most newspapers do still have political views, which can be centre-right or left-leaning.

37
00:03:54.434 --> 00:04:02.580
But these views are mainly expressed in the newspapers' so-called editorials, which are the newspapers' own comments on political events.

38
00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:10.205
Since the 1980s, newspapers have taken on the task of being "society's watchdog" even more clearly.

39
00:04:10.155 --> 00:04:15.417
That is, they keep an eye on all kinds of holders of power on behalf of citizens.

40
00:04:15.417 --> 00:04:21.492
On most media, a story that is critical of those in power is regarded as a good news story.

41
00:04:21.492 --> 00:04:26.118
That is why people also talk about the media as "the fourth power of the state".

42
00:04:26.668 --> 00:04:37.518
In the last approximately 15 years, the political debate has increasingly moved to social media such as Facebook, X, formerly Twitter, TikTok and Instagram.

43
00:04:37.468 --> 00:04:46.731
Here the politicians can themselves present or comment on a case instead of appearing for an interview with journalists in newspapers, on radio or on TV.

44
00:04:46.731 --> 00:04:53.955
The population can likewise comment themselves, ask questions, write or share a post about political topics.

45
00:04:53.955 --> 00:05:03.555
On the one hand, the emergence of social media has given citizens easier and more direct access to communicate with each other and with the politicians.

46
00:05:03.555 --> 00:05:11.843
On the other hand, debate on social media often takes place without the journalists having the opportunity to ask critical questions.

47
00:05:11.843 --> 00:05:16.920
That can contribute to weakening the media's opportunity to be "society's watchdog".

48
00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:23.207
Now let's bring democracy home: democracy in everyday life, citizenship.

49
00:05:23.157 --> 00:05:26.895
Democracy has spread to many parts of Danish society.

50
00:05:26.895 --> 00:05:32.932
For example, there is a tradition that citizens can help set the framework for their everyday lives.

51
00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:42.345
For example in the local school, where parents and pupils can discuss the school's affairs in the school board, or in class councils and pupil councils.

52
00:05:42.345 --> 00:05:49.557
This democracy in everyday life is also found on the boards of housing associations and cooperative associations.

53
00:05:49.557 --> 00:06:00.520
There is also a long tradition of employees having insight into, and influence on, conditions at their workplace through a so-called cooperation committee, a samarbejdsudvalg.

54
00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:05.274
In all of these contexts, people work according to democratic principles.

55
00:06:05.824 --> 00:06:10.836
Denmark is also characterised by a rich associational and organisational life.

56
00:06:10.786 --> 00:06:16.424
There are not many other countries where people are members of as many associations and organisations.

57
00:06:16.424 --> 00:06:25.674
About 90 percent of the Danish population are members of at least one association, and about 35 percent have done voluntary work through an association.

58
00:06:25.674 --> 00:06:31.249
On average, every Dane is a member of three to four associations or organisations.

59
00:06:31.249 --> 00:06:42.624
They can be organisations on the labour market, or leisure organisations where the members gather around a common hobby or interest such as football, scouting, birdlife, chess or ceramics.

60
00:06:42.624 --> 00:06:52.140
Others are members of organisations that want to promote particular causes, for example protecting the environment, like the Danish Society for Nature Conservation.

61
00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:59.602
For many citizens, association life and everyday democracy also work as a training in democracy.

62
00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:06.615
Here you discuss matters with each other, gather knowledge, test arguments, make compromises and vote on proposals.

63
00:07:06.615 --> 00:07:12.790
Participants in that way become used to trying to find reasonable solutions to the problems they have gathered around.

64
00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:22.518
Life in the associations and democracy in everyday life give citizens, in other words, a practical knowledge of democratic values and principles.

65
00:07:23.068 --> 00:07:27.593
The most widespread democratic activity is voting when there is an election.

66
00:07:27.543 --> 00:07:39.118
You are not obliged to vote, but the vast majority do so anyway, and voter turnout, that is how many of those with the right to vote actually vote, is quite high compared with most other countries.

67
00:07:39.118 --> 00:07:46.118
It is particularly high when there are elections to the Folketing, an average of 86 percent since the year 2000.

68
00:07:46.118 --> 00:07:59.712
It is somewhat lower when voting for municipal and regional elections, an average of 72 percent since 2000, and a bit lower still for the European Parliament, an average of 58 percent since 2000.

69
00:08:00.262 --> 00:08:04.562
The generally high turnout in Denmark can have several explanations.

70
00:08:04.512 --> 00:08:09.499
One of them is that Danes are brought up to democracy as children and young people.

71
00:08:09.499 --> 00:08:14.762
They learn democratic values early, and voting is a crucial democratic value.

72
00:08:14.762 --> 00:08:20.012
This happens, among other places, in the folkeskole and in the youth educations.

73
00:08:20.012 --> 00:08:29.449
Another explanation is that the strong tradition of being part of associations strengthens citizens' democratic values and ability to cope in organisations.

74
00:08:29.449 --> 00:08:35.274
Finally, it may also matter that democracy in Denmark has from the beginning been a popular project.

75
00:08:35.274 --> 00:08:39.687
Democracy was carried forward not least by the farmers and the workers.

76
00:08:39.687 --> 00:08:45.662
They were organised early in the great movements that became the parties Venstre and the Social Democrats.

77
00:08:45.662 --> 00:08:50.999
This has created a tradition of democratic participation that still marks Denmark.

78
00:08:50.999 --> 00:08:54.974
Danes today are generally very satisfied with their democracy.

79
00:08:54.974 --> 00:09:00.499
They have trust in the institutions of democracy, state, region and municipality.

80
00:09:00.499 --> 00:09:03.749
But not necessarily in the elected political leaders.

81
00:09:03.749 --> 00:09:06.594
Trust in the politicians goes up and down.

82
00:09:07.144 --> 00:09:12.281
The population's political participation is high compared with many other countries.

83
00:09:12.231 --> 00:09:16.744
But over a longer period, participation has changed character.

84
00:09:16.744 --> 00:09:23.381
Fewer people today are members of a political party, even though anyone can become a member of one if they want.

85
00:09:23.381 --> 00:09:30.369
And the so-called grassroots movements play a smaller role today than they did in the 1970s and 1980s.

86
00:09:30.369 --> 00:09:34.794
Instead, new ways of expressing political views have been created.

87
00:09:34.794 --> 00:09:46.731
It can be by signing a political declaration, a petition, by giving money to a political or humanitarian cause, or by taking political or ethical considerations as a consumer.

88
00:09:46.731 --> 00:09:52.572
Many also take part in political discussions on social media such as X and Facebook on the internet.

89
00:09:53.122 --> 00:09:55.522
Here is your recap for Episode Six.

90
00:09:55.472 --> 00:10:05.747
Sixty years ago voters were loyal to a class party: workers voted Social Democrats, farmers Venstre, the self-employed and middle class the Conservatives.

91
00:10:05.747 --> 00:10:08.084
Today voters switch parties more.

92
00:10:08.084 --> 00:10:17.234
Privately employed vote more centre-right; publicly employed more Social Democrats and the left; men more centre-right, women more left.

93
00:10:17.234 --> 00:10:19.772
Parties are not mentioned in the constitution.

94
00:10:19.772 --> 00:10:31.034
Around 1950 about 650,000 were party members, almost one in four voters; in 2024 about 106,000, about one in forty.

95
00:10:31.034 --> 00:10:36.522
The Social Democrats have about 27,000 members and Venstre about 20,000.

96
00:10:36.522 --> 00:10:42.359
A member of the Folketing is bound only by personal conviction, and may become a løsgænger.

97
00:10:42.359 --> 00:10:44.959
The Speaker is Søren Gade from Venstre.

98
00:10:44.959 --> 00:10:50.622
LO is now FH, and the Agricultural Council is now Landbrug og Fødevarer.

99
00:10:50.622 --> 00:11:01.472
Newspapers freed themselves from parties in the 1960s, became society's watchdog from the 1980s, and the media is called the fourth power of the state.

100
00:11:01.472 --> 00:11:08.134
About 90 percent are members of at least one association, and about 35 percent do voluntary work.

101
00:11:08.134 --> 00:11:16.806
Turnout: Folketing 86 percent, municipal and regional 72 percent, European Parliament 58 percent.

102
00:11:17.356 --> 00:11:19.956
Now it is time for your exam practice.

103
00:11:19.906 --> 00:11:24.818
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

104
00:11:24.818 --> 00:11:32.304
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

105
00:11:32.854 --> 00:11:34.104
Question 1.

106
00:11:34.054 --> 00:11:39.329
Who does DA represent when collective agreements are negotiated on the Danish labour market?

107
00:11:39.329 --> 00:11:41.616
Option A: The employers.

108
00:11:41.616 --> 00:11:43.716
Option B: The government.

109
00:11:43.716 --> 00:11:46.146
Option C: The employees.
[DA] Hvem repræsenterer DA, når der skal forhandles kollektive overenskomster på det danske arbejds- marked?  A: Arbejdsgiverne  |  B: Regeringen  |  C: Lønmodtagerne

110
00:11:52.696 --> 00:11:55.171
The answer is A: The employers.

111
00:11:55.121 --> 00:11:57.948
It appeared in the June 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Arbejdsgiverne

112
00:11:58.498 --> 00:12:00.261
That's the end of this part.

113
00:12:00.211 --> 00:12:01.278
Well done.

